.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-token - Print the stored session token, or suggest logging in


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 token [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the JWT session token saved by the last 'rail0 auth login' for the
current gateway (~/.cache/rail0/tokens/), together with
its expiry. If no valid token is stored, it suggests logging in instead.

.PP
This is the singular session token — not to be confused with 'rail0 tokens',
which lists the gateway's accepted payment tokens.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for token


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 token
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0(1)\fP
